# Page composition

## Purpose

Make every page readable before anyone draws it: a grid the reader can follow
without help, panel sizes that say which moment matters, and room for the
words planned into the layout.

## Scope

Layout layer. Applies to the `layout` block and the panel `size`, `tier`, and
`order` fields of every `page.yaml`, to thumbnails, and to any review of a
rendered page's structure. Used by `/page-plan`, `/thumbnails`, and
`/smart-page`. Format-specific layout (page turns, spreads, scroll rhythm)
lives in `paged/` and `webtoon/`.

## Core Principles

- The page is designed as a whole first and as panels second. A reader sees
  the full page for a moment before reading panel one.
- Size is emphasis. The largest panel on a page is the moment the page
  exists for.
- One reading path. If two readers could follow the panels in different
  orders, the layout is wrong, however good it looks.
- Lettering is part of the composition. A layout with no room for its text
  is unfinished.

## Grids and tiers

- A page is a stack of tiers read top to bottom. `layout.tiers` lists the
  panels per tier (`[1, 2, 1]` is a wide panel, two panels side by side, and
  a wide panel), and its values MUST sum to the number of panels.
  `layout.grid` says the same thing in words for a human reader.
- Defaults: 2 to 4 tiers per page and at most 3 panels in a tier. A fourth
  panel in a tier is acceptable for a deliberate run of small beats and is
  noted in `layout.notes`.
- A panel SHOULD stay inside one tier. A tall panel beside two stacked
  panels invites two reading paths. When a layout needs one, `layout.grid`
  describes it, `layout.reading_order` fixes the path, and the art gives the
  reader a cue: an overlapping balloon, a staggered gutter, or a figure whose
  gesture points to the next panel.
- A project that works on a fixed grid (six equal panels, nine equal panels)
  states it in the style bible. Pages then depart from the grid only for a
  stated reason.

## Panel count and density

- Default range: 4 to 6 panels on a page. Default ceiling: 9. A page above
  the ceiling MUST be split or its beats merged.
- Action reads best at 3 to 5 panels with one dominant panel. Conversation
  holds 5 to 7 when the panels are simple. Counts of 8 or 9 work on a
  regular grid and almost nowhere else.
- Count the words as well as the panels. A six-panel page at 40 words per
  panel is denser than a nine-panel silent page (see
  `lettering/balloons-and-captions.md` for the word budgets).
- These numbers are defaults. `project.yaml` has no field for them, so a
  project with different limits records them in the layout section of
  `style/style-bible.md`, and a single page that breaks them says why in
  `layout.notes`.

## Size and emphasis

- Every panel declares `size`: `splash`, `wide`, `large`, `medium`, `small`,
  or `inset`. The value is relative to the other panels of the same page.
- Each page SHOULD have one dominant panel, the one that carries the page's
  `purpose`. Two panels of equal top weight split the reader's attention.
- A page of equal panels is a choice for steady rhythm (see `pacing.md`).
  It MUST NOT be the default for a page that contains the turning beat of
  its scene.
- `small` panels hold one face, one hand, or one object. A small panel that
  needs a full figure and a line of dialogue is the wrong size.

## Splash and inset panels

- A splash is one panel that takes the whole page (`tiers: [1]`,
  `size: splash`). Reserve it for an establishing view, a reveal, or the
  climax of the issue. Default budget: one splash per scene at most, and no
  two splash pages in a row.
- A splash still needs a single drawable moment and a reading path for its
  lettering. Size does not excuse a crowded image.
- An inset is a small panel placed inside a larger host panel. It counts as
  a panel of the host's tier in `layout.tiers`, it has its own `order`, and
  `layout.grid` names its host.
- An inset MUST NOT cover a face, a hand, a held prop the story tracks, or
  the host's main moment. Use it for a detail the host panel is too wide to
  show: a hand on the flare gun, an eye, a dial.

## Gutters and bleed panels

- Gutters are the same width across a page unless a wider gap is meant to
  read as time (see `panel-transitions.md`). Default width: about 2% of the
  page width, 40 px on a 2000 px page.
- Borderless panels and overlapping panels are allowed when the reading
  path stays single. Record them in `layout.notes` so the prompt and the
  review both know the border is absent on purpose.
- A bleed panel runs its art to the edge of the page. Use it for scale and
  open space. Default: at most two bleed panels on a page, so the effect
  keeps its meaning.
- Nothing the reader needs sits in the outer edge of a bleed panel. In
  paged projects the limits come from `paged/print-specs.md`.

## Space for lettering

- Every panel with lettering reserves open area for it at layout time.
  `framing.composition` SHOULD say where that area is ("sky open top left
  for two balloons").
- Default: balloons and captions cover no more than a third of a panel's
  area. A panel that needs more is enlarged or its text is cut.
- Put the open area where the reading path wants the text: toward the top
  of the panel, and on the side where the eye enters (left for `ltr`,
  right for `rtl`).
- Stage the first speaker on the side where the eye enters. Staging and
  balloon order then agree, and no tail has to cross the panel (see
  `lettering/reading-order.md`).
- Text-heavy panels are `medium` or larger. A `small` or `inset` panel
  carries one short balloon or none.

## Validation

- `schemas/page.schema.json` requires `layout.grid`, `layout.tiers`,
  `layout.reading_order`, and a `size` from the enum on every panel.
- `scripts/check-continuity.js` fails a page whose `layout.tiers` does not
  sum to the panel count or whose `layout.reading_order` is not a
  permutation of the panels' `order` values.
- `hooks/validate-after-write.js` re-checks `page.yaml` against its schema
  on every write.
- Panel count ceilings, splash budgets, and lettering area are defaults with
  no mechanical check. The layout-artist checks them in the self-review of
  `/page-plan`, and `/thumbnails` tests the reading path on a rough image.
- Whether a rendered page has the planned panels in the planned grid is the
  `page_structure` check of the visual review. Whether the emphasis reads is
  visual review by the art-director.
